NAME
    json - POSIX JSON query and validation utility

SYNOPSIS
    json [-c] [-r] [-e] [filter] [file ...]
    json [-c] [-r] [-e] -f filter-file [file ...]
    json --check [file ...]

DESCRIPTION
    json extracts values from JSON documents by evaluating a small, read-only
    subset of the jq filter syntax. It is a standalone POSIX shell command and
    does not require jq, Node.js, Python, an initialized m environment, or
    non-POSIX utilities.

    With no filter, '.' selects each complete JSON input. With no input files,
    standard input is read. With multiple file operands, each is read
    independently, in order. A '-' file operand selects standard input.
    A stream may contain multiple JSON values, which are processed separately.

    The supported filter language includes '.', '.key', '.["any key"]',
    '.[0]', '.[-1]', '.[]', chained selectors, optional '?' access, '|' to
    compose filters, and ',' to produce multiple alternatives. ',' binds more
    tightly than '|'. Only read-only structural queries are implemented;
    jq's expressions, functions, arithmetic and mutations are not supported.

    A missing key or out-of-range array index returns JSON null. An empty
    collection iterated with '.[]' returns no values. Access to incompatible
    non-null types is an error unless '?' is used. Object member names are
    matched after decoding JSON Unicode escapes. Duplicate object names use
    the last value, retaining the first occurrence's position in output.

    JSON input is validated strictly, including UTF-8, all JSON tokens,
    nested structures, and trailing content. Escaped unpaired surrogate
    code units are allowed by the JSON ABNF; raw string output replaces them
    with valid Unicode replacement characters. Numeric lexemes are preserved
    without floating-point conversion. To avoid leaking partial output from
    malformed input, output is held in private temporary storage until all
    input has been validated.

OPTIONS
    -c, --compact-output
        Output compact JSON, one result per line. Default JSON output uses
        two-space indentation for objects and arrays. NOTE: -c means compact
        output, not validation; the old -c interpretation was removed.

    -r, --raw-output
        Output decoded JSON string values without surrounding JSON quotes;
        other JSON result types retain JSON formatting.

    -e, --exit-status
        Base status on the last result: 0 for values other than false/null;
        1 for false or null; 4 for no results.

    -f, --from-file <filter-file>
        Read the filter expression from a file instead of a filter argument.

    --check
        Validate exactly one JSON value in each input stream without
        producing output. A missing or additional JSON value is an error. No filter or
        formatting/exit-status options may accompany --check.

    --
        Stop option processing. The first remaining argument still denotes
        a filter (unless --check or -f already selected the mode/filter).

    -h, --help
        Print brief usage information.

OPERANDS
    filter
        Optional read-only jq-style filter; default '.'.
    file
        Optional path to an input file; '-' means standard input.

ENVIRONMENT
    TMPDIR
        Directory for private staging; defaults to /tmp.

DEPENDENCIES
    POSIX sh, od, awk, mkdir, rm, rmdir and cat.

EXIT STATUS
    0    Successful query/validation, or -e with true/non-null last result.
    1    -e last result false or null.
    2    Invalid CLI arguments or option.
    3    Invalid/unsupported filter syntax.
    4    -e with no results.
    7    Could not create private temporary storage.
    8    Input/filter file unavailable.
    9    Input/filter file read failure.
    10   Invalid JSON lexical token or escape.
    11   Invalid UTF-8.
    12   Invalid JSON structure, or --check without exactly one value.
    13   Query type error.
    15   Internal parser/byte stream error.
    16   Output write failure.
    17   Standard-input read failure.
    18   Filter file unavailable.
    19   Filter file read failure.
    20   Filter staging failure.
    129+ Termination by handled signal.

EXAMPLES
    printf '%s\n' '{"user":{"name":"Ada"}}' | json -r '.user.name'
        Ada

    json -r '.servers[].host' config.json
        Each server hostname on a separate line.

    json -c '.packages[0]' packages.json
        Compact JSON for the first package.

    json -e '.enabled' config.json >/dev/null
        Exit 0 if the final value is neither false nor null.

    json --check config.json
        Validate the entire file without printing it.

SEE ALSO
    jq(1) (CLI/filter-language reference), RFC 8259, ECMA-404.
